          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "Here are reputable, established TCM options in Singapore (as of July 2026). I\u2019ve grouped them by type so you can pick based on what you need.\n\nHospital\u2011linked/integrated clinics\n- Raffles Chinese Medicine (Raffles Medical Group) \u2014 MOH\u2011registered TCM physicians working alongside Western specialists; good for integrated care such as women\u2019s health, pain and cancer supportive care. ([rafflesmedicalgroup.com](https://www.rafflesmedicalgroup.com/services/chinese-medicine/?utm_source=openai))\n- Thomson Chinese Medicine (Thomson Medical) \u2014 private\u2011hospital setting with TCMPB\u2011licensed physicians; broad women\u2019s and family care services. ([thomsonmedical.com](https://www.thomsonmedical.com/centres/chinese-medicine?utm_source=openai))\n- Bao Zhong Tang TCM Centre (Novena Specialist Center, HealthCity Novena) \u2014 premium clinic in the Novena medical hub, with bilingual physicians registered under TCMPB. ([baozhongtang.com.sg](https://baozhongtang.com.sg/our-clinic/?utm_source=openai))\n\nLarge, long\u2011running clinic groups (island\u2011wide access)\n- Eu Yan Sang TCM Clinic \u2014 well\u2011known brand with multiple clinics and a medical centre; convenient central and heartland locations. ([sg.euyansangclinic.com](https://sg.euyansangclinic.com/clinics?utm_source=openai))\n- Ma Kuang TCM \u2014 one of the biggest networks here (founded 1999) with 30\u2011plus clinics and TuiNa centres. ([makuang.com.sg](https://makuang.com.sg/en/about-us/contact-us/?utm_source=openai))\n- PULSE TCM \u2014 modern clinics focused on fertility, pain and rehab; ~15 locations near major MRTs. ([pulsetcm.sg](https://pulsetcm.sg/clinics/?utm_source=openai))\n- Kin Teck Tong \u2014 home\u2011grown brand since 1967; blends traditional therapies with some modern modalities. ([kintecktong.com.sg](https://www.kintecktong.com.sg/kin-teck-tong?utm_source=openai))\n- Oriental Remedies Group \u2014 patient\u2011centred TCM enhanced with technology; multi\u2011site and bilingual team. ([orientalremediesgroup.com](https://www.orientalremediesgroup.com/tcm-clinic/about-us/?utm_source=openai))\n\nCommunity, charity and teaching clinics (low cost/nominal fees; longer waits possible)\n- Singapore Thong Chai Medical Institution \u2014 historic charitable TCM clinic (est. 1867) offering free consultations, HQ at 50 Chin Swee Road with branch network. ([stcmi.org.sg](https://www.stcmi.org.sg/about-us/organization-profile/?utm_source=openai))\n- Singapore Chung Hwa Medical Institution (run by Singapore Chinese Physicians\u2019 Association) \u2014 multiple branches; physicians are TCMPB\u2011registered; nominal fees. ([zhonghuayiyuan.com](https://www.zhonghuayiyuan.com/services-2/registration-hour-charges/?utm_source=openai))\n- NTU Chinese Medicine Clinic \u2014 TCMPB\u2011approved teaching clinic established in 2009 under Nanyang Technological University. ([ntu.edu.sg](https://www.ntu.edu.sg/tcm/about?utm_source=openai))\n\nHow to choose (quick checks)\n- Verify the physician is registered: use the TCMPB public register before booking. ([tcmpb.gov.sg](https://www.tcmpb.gov.sg/for-professionals/registered-tcm-practitioners/?utm_source=openai))\n- Consider your goal: integrated care with hospital access (Raffles/Thomson/Bao Zhong Tang), broad access and convenience (Eu Yan Sang/Ma Kuang/PULSE/Kin Teck Tong/Oriental Remedies), or affordable community care (Thong Chai/Chung Hwa).\n- Bring your medication list and inform your GP/specialist if you\u2019re combining TCM with ongoing medical treatment. General guidance from MOH notes that TCMPB regulates practitioners and HSA regulates proprietary Chinese medicines. ([hpp.moh.gov.sg](https://hpp.moh.gov.sg/tcm-practitioners/about/?utm_source=openai))\n\nIf you share what you\u2019re seeking help for (e.g., pain, fertility, digestion, postpartum, sports injury), I can narrow this to a few specific clinics and physicians near your neighbourhood and help with first\u2011visit expectations and fees."
